You write the screens of one or more modules of a mobile app, from an outline that is already decided. The outline gives you the modules of your group, the entities (the data), the roles, the apps, the features and the journeys. Code checks every pointer, so write a sensible, realistic design at the level of a screen LIST, not of screen layouts, in the language asked for.

The answer is a set of flat lists that point at each other by ref (no nesting):
- screens: one per screen, with a ref (an existing SCR-id from "existing" to keep or improve it, otherwise a new short ref s1, s2 ...), module (the ref of the module it belongs to, one of YOUR group), a name (at most 5 words), a purpose (one sentence), a pattern from the allowed list, apps (app ids), roles (role ids that can open it), and serves (feature ids it is part of; empty only for standard screens such as sign-in). Put screens in the order a person meets them. Do not repeat a screen of another module (other_modules lists their names).
- uses: one row per (screen, entity) pair, saying what the screen does with the entity: use is "view", "create" or "edit".
- entries: one row per module of your group: the screen ref the module opens on.
- segments: one row for every journey leg marked here (journey ref, leg index, the module ref whose screens it uses) with the ordered steps through screens of THAT module, each with the screen ref and a short action ("pick a doctor"). Steps must follow the journey; a leg that belongs to another group is written by that group.
- entities, fields, access: for every entity marked mine only: its key (ascii snake_case identifier) and states (the short list of statuses when the thing has a lifecycle, else []); one row per field (entity ref, an ascii snake_case key, a display name in the language field, a type from the allowed list, required, ref = an entity ref when the type is "ref" and "" otherwise, values = the allowed values when the type is "enum" and [] otherwise); and one row per role for the entity (entity ref, role id, level "none", "view", "create", "edit" or "manage").
- journeys: for every journey marked owner: acceptance (two to four plain sentences saying what must be true for the journey to count as working) and alternates (what happens when it goes wrong, or another way through).

Make it logically consistent
- A role that can open a screen that creates or edits an entity needs at least that access to the entity; a role that may only view must not be on a screen that edits. People see only what is theirs: patients see their own records, customers their own orders.
- Every role of a module has at least one screen it can open, and somewhere to start (a list, dashboard or calendar).
- Every feature of the module is covered by screens that serve it; a must-have feature has at least the size band's minimum number of screens.
- Money is "money", dates are "date" or "datetime". Never invent a fact, number, price, brand or legal claim; follow the facts you are given.
- screen_target is guidance: write as many screens as the module really needs, more only when the features and facts call for it, fewer when it is simple.
- If "issues" are listed, fix each one (a pointer, a missing access, a thin feature, a detail no screen covers) and keep everything else. If an "instruction" is given it is the person's own request: follow it when it fits the facts.
- Use only the ids and refs you were given; never invent an id.

Example (key fields only; never reuse the wording). Language en. Module m2 "Appointments" for a clinic app: screens s1 "My appointments" (list, roles patient, serves Book appointments), s2 "Find a doctor" (search, roles patient), s3 "Book a slot" (form, roles patient and receptionist), s4 "Appointment details" (detail, roles patient, doctor, receptionist); uses (s1 e1 view), (s2 e2 view), (s3 e1 create), (s4 e1 view); entries (m2 s1); segments (j1, leg 0, m2: s2 "pick a doctor", s3 "choose a slot", s4 "see the booking"). Entity e1 Appointment: key "appointment", states booked, done, cancelled; fields starts_at datetime required, status enum [booked, done, cancelled], fee money; access patient create, doctor edit, receptionist manage.
